      <h1><span class="chrome-text">Designing an Antenna for Greenland</span></h1>
      <p class="post-subtitle">Practical Lessons for an Ultra-Wideband Radiometer</p>
      <div class="post-byline"><span><strong>Dom Belgiovane, PhD</strong></span><span>Thursday, November 5, 2026</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="post container-narrow uwbrad">
    <figure class="wide hero" style="max-width:710px;margin-top:0">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/field-wing-2016.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/field-wing-2016.jpg" width="710" height="533" alt="View over the DC-3 wing of snow-covered mountains during the 2016 UWBRAD campaign."></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Over the wing of the DC-3 during the 2016 UWBRAD campaign. Photo: Dom Belgiovane.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>How do you take the temperature of an ice sheet hundreds of meters below the surface, without drilling a hole?</p>
      <p>That was the question behind UWBRAD, the Ultra-Wideband Software Defined Microwave Radiometer. The project was led by Joel Johnson at The Ohio State University and funded through NASA's Earth Science Technology Office Instrument Incubator Program. As a graduate research associate at the OSU ElectroScience Laboratory, my job was the antenna. I designed and built the conical log spiral antenna (CLSA), including the balun and the radome, and I measured its gain pattern at Ohio State. It ended up flying out of the bottom of a DC-3 over Greenland.</p>

      <h2>The problem: one antenna, four to one bandwidth</h2>
      <p>Today the only measured information about ice sheet internal temperature comes from a small number of deep ice core sites. Earlier studies showed that brightness temperatures measured across 500 to 2000 MHz carry information about temperature at different depths inside the ice. UWBRAD was built to test that idea from an aircraft.</p>
      <p>That puts unusual demands on the antenna. A radiometer compares brightness temperature across frequency, so the antenna cannot quietly change what it is looking at as the frequency changes. If the beam narrows at the top of the band and broadens at the bottom, each channel sees a different patch of ice, and the spectrum mixes frequency with footprint. Over a four to one bandwidth, that is easy to get wrong.</p>
      <p>There was a second problem. 500 to 2000 MHz is a crowded part of the spectrum, with only a small portion protected for science. Radio frequency interference was a major concern for the whole team, and the receiver was designed around detecting and filtering it in software. The antenna had to be clean and predictable so that the rest of the system could do that work.</p>

      <h2>What I built: a conical log spiral</h2>
      <p>The UWBRAD antenna is a conical log spiral design with three parts: an internal balun feed, the conical antenna elements, and a protective radome. I designed all three.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide paper" style="max-width:984px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/fig2-balun-clsa-radome.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/fig2-balun-clsa-radome.jpg" width="984" height="716" alt="Fig. 2 from the TGRS 2018 paper: a) the internal tapered balun board, b) the conical log spiral antenna, and c) the radome over the antenna."></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Images of a) internal balun board, b) conical log spiral antenna, and c) radome over antenna. From M. J. Andrews et al., "The Ultrawideband Software-Defined Microwave Radiometer: Instrument Description and Initial Campaign Results," IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 56, no. 10, pp. 5923-5935, 2018, Fig. 2. © 2018 IEEE.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>A conical log spiral is a natural fit for this job. The geometry scales with frequency, so the active region simply moves along the cone as frequency changes, and the radiation pattern stays roughly the same. It radiates circular polarization from its tip. We used right hand circular polarization (RHCP), which simplified the ultra-wideband design and still met the model requirements for retrieving internal ice sheet temperatures.</p>
      <p>The design goal was near constant gain, and so a near constant footprint for a given platform altitude, from 500 to 2000 MHz. The design was simulated with the aircraft frame included, not just in free space. On the aircraft, the antenna deploys out of the bottom through a "periscope" system and points to nadir in level flight.</p>
      <p>I built two antennas. One went on an engineering model that the team, with our partners at IFAC-CNR in Florence, tested on a tower at Dome-C in Antarctica. The other was the flight antenna for Greenland.</p>

      <h2>Measured performance</h2>
      <p>A frequency-independent antenna is only frequency independent on paper. The balun, the finite cone, the feed region, the radome and the mounting all add their own frequency dependence. So I measured the antennas at Ohio State, in an anechoic chamber, and compared them with simulation.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide paper" style="max-width:1031px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/fig3-antenna-gain.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/010-uwbrad-greenland/images/fig3-antenna-gain.jpg" width="1031" height="429" alt="Fig. 3 from the TGRS 2018 paper: measured and simulated RHCP gain (solid) and LHCP gain (dashed) versus frequency from about 0.3 to 2.2 GHz for the Greenland antenna (red) and the Antarctica antenna (green), with simulation in black. RHCP gain sits near 10 dB across the band, with cross-pol well below it."></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Measured and simulated UWBRAD antenna gains (red lines for Greenland campaign). From M. J. Andrews et al., IEEE Trans. Geosci. Remote Sens., vol. 56, no. 10, pp. 5923-5935, 2018, Fig. 3. © 2018 IEEE.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>The key result in plain numbers, from the TGRS 2018 paper:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>About 10 dB RHCP gain</strong> across the frequencies of interest for the Greenland antenna, with a corresponding <strong>half-power beamwidth of about 60 degrees</strong>.</li>
        <li>At the nominal <strong>500 m</strong> altitude, that gives a circular footprint of <strong>about 0.6 km</strong> in diameter, about <strong>7.5 seconds</strong> to cross at the nominal <strong>150 kts</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Radome loss of 0 to 0.5 dB</strong> and <strong>balun loss of 0.1 to 0.4 dB</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Total measured losses of 1 to 4 dB</strong> during the campaign (radome, balun, mismatch and cable), with <strong>2.5 to 3.5 dB typical</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That last item is worth a pause. In a radiometer, loss in front of the receiver is not just lost signal. It adds its own thermal emission and has to be calibrated out. The team used flights over open water, with known temperature and salinity, to determine the losses of the antenna and cable and convert antenna temperatures into scene brightness. A measured, well-characterized antenna made that step tractable.</p>

      <h2>Into the field</h2>
      <p>UWBRAD flew two Greenland campaigns. The first, in September 2016, was the shorter debut: install the instrument, prove the antenna and receiver in the air, and bring home the first ultra-wideband spectra. We came back for a longer campaign in September 2017, the science flights that reached the ice sheet interior and the sea ice the team had planned for.</p>

      <h3>September 2016: the debut</h3>
      <p>The 2016 campaign ran September 12 to 15. The system was installed on a DC-3 Basler aircraft supplied by Kenn Borek Airlines. Mark Andrews and I went north with the instrument. There were two flights of note: a transit on September 12 from Calgary toward Thule Air Base, with spectrogram results reported for the leg to Cambridge Bay, Canada, and a campaign flight out of Thule on September 15.</p>
    </div>

