    <div class="measure">
      <p>The September 15 path crossed sea scenes, coastal rock near Qaanaaq, and then headed east onto the Greenland ice sheet through regions where ablation, wet snow, percolation and dry snow are expected to dominate thermal emission from the upper ice. Phys.org covered the result: the 2016 flights provided about 10 hours of the first ultra-wideband microwave radiometer measurements of geophysical scenes including ice sheets (<a href="https://phys.org/news/2017-09-method-remotely-ice-sheet-subsurface.html">Phys.org, September 2017</a>). OSU's College of Engineering also wrote up the trip (<a href="https://engineering.osu.edu/news/2016/10/researchers-measure-global-ice-sheets-sky">OSU, October 2016</a>).</p>

      <h3>September 2017: the longer science campaign</h3>
      <p>I also flew the September 2017 Greenland campaign. That was the longer one. Calgary was again the aircraft base of operations (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2018.8517321">IGARSS 2018</a>). In Greenland we flew out of Thule and Kangerlussuaq. The tracks covered northwestern Greenland, the Camp Century to NEEM to NGRIP interior line, and a far-north leg over Arctic sea ice in the Lincoln Sea, up toward the North Pole but not quite there (<a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.1029216">Jezek et al., Frontiers 2022</a>; <a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2020.3043954">Yardim et al., TGRS 2022</a>). The instrument observed the ice sheet as well as sea ice, the ocean surface and land regions during the transit to and from Calgary (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/IGARSS.2018.8517321">IGARSS 2018</a>).</p>
    </div>

