    <div class="measure">
      <p>Along the Camp Century to NEEM to NGRIP segment, the team later retrieved vertical temperature profiles and compared them with borehole measurements at those three campsites (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2020.3043954">Yardim et al., TGRS 2022</a>). Over Lincoln Sea sea ice, UWBRAD spectra were used to invert for sea ice thickness and salinity (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2019.2922163">Jezek et al., TGRS 2019</a>). For that 2017 Greenland campaign I received the Old Polar Explorer Award from the Byrd Polar and Climate Research Center at The Ohio State University.</p>

      <h2>What the flights showed</h2>
      <p>UWBRAD had three main objectives: observe ice sheet brightness temperatures from 500 to 2000 MHz, identify and mitigate RFI in software in real time and in post-processing, and retrieve internal ice sheet temperatures from the measured spectra with models. The September 2016 flights were the airborne debut. The September 2017 flights were the longer science campaign that put those objectives to work over the ice sheet interior and sea ice.</p>

      <h3>2016: instrument, RFI and calibration</h3>
      <p>On the September 12 transit, spectrograms show a hard RFI environment near Calgary and Edmonton, then more usable data after Lake Athabasca. RFI below about 1 GHz was generally more frequent and more powerful. On the September 15 Thule flight, heavy RFI appears in raw Level 0 stretches; Level 1 (time domain) and Level 2 (frequency domain) processing remove much of it. The TGRS 2018 paper reports RFI magnitudes ranging up to 1 million K in these unprotected bands.</p>
      <p>Flights over water on both days provided external calibration. Water temperature and salinity give an expected scene brightness, which the team used to determine antenna and cable losses and convert antenna temperatures into scene brightness. That is why a measured, band-wide antenna characterization matters: the aperture is part of the calibration chain, and its near-constant gain and footprint across 500 to 2000 MHz keep each channel looking at roughly the same patch of ice or water.</p>
      <p>After RFI processing, the integrated time series become significantly smoother and appear to correspond to geophysical expectations, though some residual RFI remains in the full-spectrum product and is reduced further in individual channels. The TGRS 2018 paper focuses on the instrument, calibration and RFI. A more detailed look at the ice sheet brightness temperature spectra over the different snow zones is in the companion paper by Jezek and coauthors.</p>
      <p>The campaign was not without hardware trouble. The RF frontend failed twice: once shortly after takeoff from Cambridge Bay on the transit toward Greenland, and again on the backup unit after about 90 minutes of collecting data in Greenland. Both failures damaged the initial amplifiers and appeared to be caused by an excessively high input signal, possibly from static discharge in the antenna or from high power radars in the area. A bandpass filter, power limiter and ESD diode were added at the input, and a second campaign to observe the central Greenland ice sheet was successfully carried out in September 2017.</p>

      <h3>2017: ice sheet temperature and sea ice</h3>
      <p>The 2017 spectra over northwestern Greenland showed strong spatial and spectral variations that correlate with internal ice sheet temperature. Along the Camp Century to NEEM to NGRIP segment, Yardim and coauthors retrieved vertical temperature profiles with a sequential Bayesian estimator, using borehole measurements at the three campsites as priors. The retrieved profiles show reasonable behaviors and demonstrate the potential of ultra-wideband microwave radiometry for remotely sensing internal ice sheet temperatures (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2020.3043954">Yardim et al., TGRS 2022</a>).</p>
      <p>At the northern apex of the 2017 tracks, over Arctic sea ice in the Lincoln Sea, UWBRAD spectra were compared with thermal infrared imagery from the campaign and with nearly concurrent Sentinel-1 C-band SAR (<a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2022.1029216">Jezek et al., Frontiers 2022</a>). Spectral signatures were associated with thick multiyear ice and thin ice, and a radiative transfer model was used to invert the spectra for sea ice thickness and salinity. Inferred thicknesses were consistent with ice thickness climatology for floes in the Lincoln Sea (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1109/TGRS.2019.2922163">Jezek et al., TGRS 2019</a>).</p>
      <p>By the TGRS 2018 paper's conclusion, UWBRAD showed in the lab and in the field that it can make stable and accurate ultra-wideband radiometric measurements from 500 to 2000 MHz in the presence of significant RFI. The 2016 debut delivered roughly 10 hours of first ultra-wideband radiometer data and a hardened instrument. The longer 2017 campaign delivered the ice sheet and sea ice science the project was built for.</p>

      <h2>The lesson: pattern stability is the spec</h2>
      <p>If you are designing an ultra-wideband aperture, gain at boresight is not enough. What I took away from UWBRAD:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Write the requirement as pattern stability across the band.</strong> Gain, beamwidth and polarization purity at the bottom, middle and top of the band, not one headline number.</li>
        <li><strong>Simulate the installed antenna.</strong> We modeled the antenna with the aircraft frame because the platform is part of the aperture.</li>
        <li><strong>Measure with and without the radome, and measure the balun loss.</strong> Small losses that vary over frequency matter, especially in a receive-only, calibration-driven system like a radiometer.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify on a range, across the full band.</strong> "Frequency independent" is a design intent. A measured gain curve that sits on the simulation from 0.5 to 2 GHz is the evidence.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>More on UWBRAD</h2>
      <p>The antenna design itself is in my APSURSI 2016 paper with Chi-Chih Chen and Joel Johnson. The instrument, calibration and RFI work is in the TGRS 2018 paper led by Mark Andrews. The 2017 campaign overview is in our IGARSS 2018 paper. Ice sheet subsurface temperature retrieval from the 2017 flight is in Yardim et al., TGRS 2022. The Lincoln Sea sea ice work is in Jezek et al., TGRS 2019, and the combined 2016/2017 flight-track map is in Jezek et al., Frontiers 2022. Ken Jezek at the Byrd Polar and Climate Research Center and Giovanni Macelloni and Marco Brogioni at IFAC-CNR are among the coauthors across these papers.</p>
      <p>If you're working on a wideband or ultra-wideband aperture and want a second set of eyes on pattern stability, installed performance or a measurement plan, I do paid one-hour wideband aperture design reviews. Book one here: <a href="https://calendar.app.google/BkzA1rurMrozK5Ta9">https://calendar.app.google/BkzA1rurMrozK5Ta9</a></p>

